  "id": "Compound::DB00563 - Disease::DOID:5041",
  "verbalization": "Methotrexate, a well-established cytotoxic chemotherapeutic agent, is implicated in the treatment of bone cancer and esophageal cancer through a shared mechanism of action with cisplatin. The evidence suggests that Methotrexate may indirectly contribute to esophageal cancer development by influencing the progression of bone cancer, which in turn is treated with cisplatin. This pathway is supported by the lowest common ancestor relationship between Methotrexate and cisplatin, both classified as heteroatomic molecular entities, indicating a common biochemical pathway. The observed relationship between Methotrexate and esophageal cancer is thus mediated through cisplatin, which is a known agent that can induce esophageal cancer in certain contexts. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Methotrexate to play a role in esophageal cancer pathogenesis, though further mechanistic validation is required to confirm this relationship.",
